{
  "dom_id": "step-q132654",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist.",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
